Fixed: yoyo no longer treats the Debian/Ubuntu set-group sg as ast-grep. Committed as d84d59fb, with "Fixes #1131" in the title. fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full cargo test all pass.

What was wrong: yoyo checked only whether sg --version exited 0. On this runner /usr/bin/sg is util-linux's set-group command, which also exits 0, so yoyo ast foo failed with sg: unrecognized option '--pattern'. yoyo map --all also claimed it was "using ast-grep" when the symbols actually came from the regex fallback.

What changed:
• One shared check, run once per process (ast_grep_binary() in commands_ast_grep.rs). It tries ast-grep first, then sg. A binary counts only if --version exits 0 and its first line starts with the word ast-grep. Trying ast-grep first also covers ast-grep retiring the sg name.
• Both places that start ast-grep use it: the /ast search and /map's symbol scan in symbols.rs. If nothing is found, /map uses the regex extractor without starting anything. symbols.rs stays at exactly 3804 lines, so its size-limit entry didn't need updating.
• Docs: the two sentences in docs/src/usage/commands.md that said ast-grep means sg now describe the new check.

Tests:
• A table of --version outputs. The must-reject row is the util-linux line copied from this runner (sg from util-linux 2.41.3); the must-accept row is ast-grep 0.39.5.
• A direct test of the choosing logic: util-linux sg only → not installed; real ast-grep under the name sg → found; both present → ast-grep wins and sg is never tried; a failed exit doesn't count even if the output looks right.
• The old test that only asserted when sg was missing now asserts in both cases.
• An integration test puts a fake util-linux sg on PATH and checks for exit 2 and "not installed".
• Sanity check on the tests: making the version check accept anything turned the new unit test and the integration test red. The check is restored, and the tree has no leftover markers.

The built binary on this runner:
• yoyo ast foo now prints "ast-grep (sg) is not installed." and exits 2.
• yoyo map --all now ends with "6499 symbols across 253 files (using regex)".

Left alone on purpose: the "ast-grep (sg) is not installed." wording. Two integration tests check for it, and it's still accurate as the tool's name. It could be confusing on a machine where some other sg exists; if that's worth changing, it's a separate small edit.
